# Shell scripts must be LF, and this file is the reason the fleet kept finding CRLF ones.
#
# A CRLF .sh is not runnable: `set -euo pipefail` arrives as `set -euo pipefail\r`, and the
# shell reports it as an invalid option named "pipe" (measured 2026-09-25 on a fleet macOS
# host: the installer died at line 48 with `set: pipe: invalid option name`). Two installers
# were COMMITTED with CRLF, so every clone and every `update` - which is a git pull, and now
# the only update door - landed unrunnable scripts on Unix hosts. The packaged copies were
# always fine because build-package.py normalises .sh with lf_only(), which is precisely what
# hid this for as long as it lasted: the installers a reader downloads worked, and the scripts
# a host pulls did not.
#
# Deliberately NOT *.py: tinycmdr.py is a CRLF file on purpose and carries bare CRs that a
# renormalise would strip. Same for tinycmdr.cmd (CRLF is what cmd.exe wants).
*.sh      text eol=lf
*.command text eol=lf
# The extensionless launcher, the same rule for the same reason: it is a /bin/sh
# script and the one file the PATH wrapper execs. core.autocrlf=true on the
# Windows box gave it CRLF despite the LF blob, so every package built from that
# worktree shipped a launcher no Mac or Linux would run (measured 2026-09-26).
/tinycmdr text eol=lf
